      "aws_a2_4_blueprint_decoder": "AWS A2.4 blueprint decoder",
      "aws_a2_4_the_welding_symbol_explained": "AWS A2.4 — the welding symbol explained",
      "aws_a2_4_weld_symbols_are_a_language_o": "AWS A2.4 weld symbols are a language. Once fluent, you read a symbol in 2 seconds. Until then, every print is a translation exercise. (Symbols Reader module covers each symbol in interactive detail; this section drills the key reading rules.)",
      "aws_certification_trivia": "AWS certification trivia: ",
      "aws_certified_welding_inspector_prep": "AWS Certified Welding Inspector prep",
      "aws_certified_welding_inspector_requir": "AWS Certified Welding Inspector — requires combination of education + welding experience (e.g., 5 yr welding + HS = qualified; 2 yr welding + Associate degree in welding tech = qualified). Pass 3-part exam ($1,150 fee). Renew every 3 yr. Maintained by continuing education + active inspection work.",
      "aws_d1_1": "AWS D1.1",
      "aws_d1_1_asme_ix_api_1104": "AWS D1.1, ASME IX, API 1104",
      "aws_d1_1_structural_cert": "AWS D1.1 Structural Cert",
      "aws_electrode_classification_looks_lik": "AWS electrode classification looks like \"E7018.\" Each character means something:",
      "aws_performance_written_test_prep": "AWS performance + written test prep",
      "aws_position_codes_what_1f_3g_6g_mean_": "AWS Position Codes — what 1F / 3G / 6G mean on a print",
      "aws_weld_symbol_fluency": "🔣 AWS weld symbol fluency",
      "back_to_catalog": "← Back to Catalog",
      "back_to_weldlab_menu": "Back to WeldLab menu",
      "bad_parameters_visible_bad_bead": "— bad parameters → visible bad bead",
      "badge_earned_joint_reader": "🏅 Badge earned: Joint Reader",
      "base_metal": "Base metal",
      "base_metal_that_didn_t_melt_but_got_ho": "Base metal that didn't melt but got hot enough to change microstructure. The most failure-prone region of any weld.",
      "base_metal_that_didn_t_melt_but_reache": " — base metal that didn't melt but reached temperatures high enough to change grain structure + properties. ",
      "bath_iron_works": "Bath Iron Works",
      "bath_iron_works_2": "🚢 Bath Iron Works",
      "bath_iron_works_bath": "Bath Iron Works (Bath)",
      "bath_iron_works_biw_maine_s_anchor_emp": "🚢 Bath Iron Works (BIW) — Maine's anchor employer",
      "bath_iron_works_emcc_employers_apprent": "Bath Iron Works, EMCC, employers, apprenticeships",
      "bath_iron_works_hires_hundreds_of_weld": "Bath Iron Works hires hundreds of welders a year for US Navy destroyer construction. Cianbro, regional marine yards, paper mills, and pipeline contractors all post welding openings. AWS D1.1 certification + a high-school diploma can land a $50-70K job at age 19. 6G TIG cert can push it to $90K+. No four-year degree required.",
      "bath_iron_works_hiring_eastern_maine_c": "Bath Iron Works hiring, Eastern Maine Community College welding program, AWS certification ladder, salary bands by certification, apprenticeship vs trade school paths.",
      "bead_overhangs_edge": "bead overhangs edge",
      "bead_view_mode": "Bead view mode",
      "bead_width": "Bead Width",
      "below_1340_f_body_centered_cubic_low_c": "Below ~1340°F. Body-centered cubic. Low carbon solubility. Magnetic. Soft + ductile.",
      "bend_tests": "Bend tests: ",
      "best_for": "Best For",
      "better_for_fill_cap_passes": "• Better for fill + cap passes",
      "better_for_root_passes_on_high_strengt": "• Better for root passes on high-strength steel — avoids HAZ softening",
      "better_penetration": "• Better penetration",
      "between_passes_the_welder_must_chip_sl": "Between passes, the welder MUST chip slag + grind any defects + wire-brush the previous pass. Slag inclusions caused by skipping interpass cleaning are the #1 cause of pipe weld rejects in X-ray inspection.",
      "bevel_cut_for_30": "Bevel cut for 30°: ",
      "bevel_groove": "Bevel groove",
      "beyond_biw_maine_s_mid_coast_has_one_o": "Beyond BIW, Maine's mid-coast has one of the densest boatbuilding clusters in North America. Most are aluminum or steel construction; some composite. Welders here typically do TIG (aluminum + stainless), often work alongside designers + engineers, build everything from megayachts to Navy support craft.",
      "beyond_ppe_basics_welding_fume_mangane": "Beyond PPE basics: welding fume (manganese, hexavalent chromium, nickel), noise exposure + hearing loss, ergonomic injuries (back, shoulder, wrist), arc-eye + cataracts, skin cancer, hot metal burns, electrocution risks, confined space, fire watch protocols, OSHA + NIOSH regs, long-term career health planning. Welders are 30%+ more likely to develop lung cancer — not a footnote.",
      "beyond_practical_wet_welding_limits": "Beyond practical wet-welding limits",
      "big_shipyard_or_factory": "🏭 Big shipyard or factory",
      "bigger_better_visibility_4_3_minimum_4": "Bigger = better visibility. 4×3\" minimum; 4×4\" or 5×4\" preferred for pipe + structural work.",
      "biw_a_general_dynamics_company_builds_": "BIW (a General Dynamics company) builds Arleigh Burke-class destroyers (DDG-51) for the US Navy. Founded 1884. Located on the Kennebec River in Bath. Largest private employer in Maine.",
      "biw_bath_dominates_designed_structural": "BIW (Bath) dominates. Designed structural + pipefitting welds for Navy destroyers. ~6,500 employees, IAM Local S6 union. Aggressive hiring.",
      "biw_internal_training": "BIW Internal Training",
      "biw_internal_training_production_welde": "BIW Internal Training → Production welder",
      "biw_pratt_whitney_cianbro_modules": "BIW, Pratt & Whitney, Cianbro modules",
      "biw_runs_its_own_training_school_16_we": "BIW runs its own training school. ~16-week program. Paid while you train ($18-19/hr starting). After completion + qualification, you join production. Application: biwjobs.com or BIW HR (207-443-3311). HUGE entry channel — no prior welding required for some entries.",
      "biw_work_is_hard_outdoor_in_maine_wint": "BIW work is hard. Outdoor in Maine winter (heated tents help but cold). Shift work (1st/2nd/3rd, mandatory OT). Strict drug testing (Navy contract). Physical (carrying gear up + down hulls). Trade-offs are real wages, full benefits, pension, and a job that builds Navy ships.",
      "blueprint_reading": "📋 Blueprint reading",
